{%- comment -%}
  Hartwick Atelier — product identity (Wireframe 03 §02).

  The eyebrow, the Style name and the Expression, at the top of the buy panel.
  This replaces Luxe's native `title` block, which can only print product.title
  as a single line — the wireframe needs two, and they are two different things:

      LOT I                        <- hartwick.lot -> number_roman
      SHIRT 001                    <- hartwick.style -> display_name   (the h1)
      Handspun Silk-Linen,         <- hartwick.expression
      Natural Pearl Shimmer

  Terminology (CLAUDE.md): the Style is the permanent silhouette identity and the
  Expression is its fabric/dye realisation. The wireframe prints "TIDE", which is
  a legacy name — internal SKU mapping only, never customer-facing. Aloha
  confirmed this structure on 3 September 2026: "Your current naming structure is
  correct. Please use the permanent numbered Style as the heading, with the
  Expression beneath it."

  The Lot is customer-facing as a Roman numeral (Lot I), per Framework v3.

  --- EDITION -------------------------------------------------------------
  Aloha, 3 September 2026: "Please also remove Edition 07 / 25 unless this
  refers to an actual individually numbered garment. If the garments are not
  individually numbered, the opening label should simply show the confirmed Lot
  information."

  So the Edition no longer renders from a placeholder, and no longer renders
  just because a run size exists. It needs BOTH:

    hartwick.individually_numbered = true   — this Style really is numbered
                                              piece by piece, confirmed
    hartwick.edition_number        = 7      — THIS garment's own number

  Absent either, the eyebrow is the Lot alone. A run size on its own is a
  production quantity, not an edition number, and belongs in the Material
  Record as a fact — never in the opening label, and never as a badge.

  This is the page's only <h1>. The native title block must stay disabled.

  Usage (inside a main-product custom_liquid block):
    {% render 'ha-pdp-identity', ph_lot: 'Lot I', ph_expression: 'Handspun Khadi' %}
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign mf = product.metafields.hartwick

  assign lot_label = ''
  if mf.lot.value.number_roman != blank
    assign lot_label = 'Lot ' | append: mf.lot.value.number_roman
  else
    assign lot_label = ph_lot
  endif

  # An edition number is a property of an individually numbered garment. Both
  # facts have to be present and confirmed before the label can claim it.
  assign edition_label = ''
  if mf.individually_numbered == true and mf.edition_number != blank
    assign edition_label = 'Edition ' | append: mf.edition_number
    if mf.edition_size != blank
      assign edition_label = edition_label | append: ' / ' | append: mf.edition_size
    endif
  endif

  # The Style's display name is the heading. With no Style bound we fall back to
  # the product's own title rather than to wireframe copy — a real product title
  # is always better than a placeholder.
  assign style_name = product.title
  if mf.style.value.display_name != blank
    assign style_name = mf.style.value.display_name
  endif

  assign expression = mf.expression | default: ph_expression

  assign is_placeholder = false
  if mf.style.value.display_name == blank or mf.expression == blank or mf.lot.value.number_roman == blank
    assign is_placeholder = true
  endif
-%}

<div class="ha-pdp__identity">
  {%- if lot_label != blank or edition_label != blank -%}
    <p class="ha-pdp__eyebrow ha-record">
      {{- lot_label -}}
      {%- if lot_label != blank and edition_label != blank %} &middot; {% endif -%}
      {{- edition_label -}}
    </p>
  {%- endif -%}

  <h1 class="ha-pdp__title ha-identify">{{ style_name }}</h1>

  {%- if expression != blank -%}
    <p class="ha-pdp__expression ha-narrate">{{ expression }}</p>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if is_placeholder -%}
    {%- render 'ha-placeholder-note',
      show: true,
      text: 'Placeholder — Style, Expression and Lot publish from hartwick.* metafields'
    -%}
  {%- endif -%}
</div>
